JAVA vs Python 

Manual-compile
.java → (javac) → .class → (JVM) → Machine Code -> Execution

Auto-compile
.py → (Python) → .pyc → (PVM) → Execution


Java:
Translate a book once → distribute copies → readers read faster
Compile once, runs everywhere
Compiled to bytecode, then JIT-compiled (JVM JIT)
Frequently used code is converted to native machine code
Static Typing
Primitive types (int, float) are not objects
More efficient memory usage
True multi-threading


Python:
Interpreter reads and explains the book line by line
Bytecode runs everywhere 
Executes bytecode instruction by instruction (CPython)
No aggressive runtime optimization
Dynamic Typing
Everything is an object (even integers) --> More memory usage
GIL allows only one thread to execute Python bytecode at a time








